#Shell Builtin Commands

#Builtin commands are cotnained in the shell itself. When the first word of a simple command is a builtin name, the
# shell executes the command directly without invoking another program. Builtins male possible functionality that
#is difficult or impossible to be done with error utilities.
#Builtins discussed here are those which bash has inherited from the Bourne shell, and also those unique builtins
#which have been extended by bash.
#builtins that provide bash interface to the job control facilites (job control builtins), the directory stack
#(Directory stack builtins), command history (Bash History Builtins), and the programmable completion facilities.
#Many of the builtins have been extended by POSIX or Bash
#Each builtin that accepts options preceded by '-' accept '--' to signify the end of options. 
#The builtins true,false, and test do not accept options and dont treat '--' specially. 
#The builtins exit, break, continue, let, and shift accept and process arguments beginning with '-' without 
#requiring '--'. Other builtins that accept argumetns  but are not specified as accepting options interprets 
#arguments with '-' as invalid options and require '--' to prevent this interpretation.

#Bourne shell builtins
: (colon)
: [arguments]

#only expands arguments and performs redirections. Return status is zero

. (period)
. [-p path] filename [arguments]
#dot command reads and executes commands from the filename argument in the current shell context.
#If filename has no slash, then dot searches for it. If -p is provided, then dot inerprets path as a colon separated
#list of directories in which to find filename; else, dot uses the directories in PATH to find filename.
#Filename need not be an executable. In not in POSIX mode, Bash searches the current directory if filename is not found 
#in $PATH, but doesn't search current directory if -p supplied. If sourcepath option (shoptbuiltin) is turned off,
#dot does not search PATH.
# arguments supplied become positional parameters when executing filename, else positional parameters are unchanged
#with -T option enabled, dot inherits any trap on DEBUG; if it is not, any DEBUG, any DEBUG trap is saved and 
#restored around the call to dot, and dot unsets the DEBUG trap while it executes. If -T is not set, and the
#sourced file changes the DEBUG trap, the new value persists after dot compiles. The return status is the exit status
#of the last command executed from filename, or zero if no commands are executed. If filename is not found or can't
# be read, the return status is non-zero. This builtin is equivalent to source.

break
break [n]
#exit from a for, while, until, or select loop. If n is supplied, break exits the nth enclosing
#loop. n has to be more than or equal to 1. Return status is zero unless n is not greater than or equal to 1.
 cd
cd [-L] [-@] [directory]
cd -P [-e] [-@] [directory]
#changes current working directory to direcotry. If directory not given, then the value of HOME shell variable is 
#used as directory. If shell variable CDPATH exists, and directory does not begin with a slash, cd uses it as a 
#search path: cd searches each directory name in CDPATH for directory, with alternative directory names in CDPATH 
#separated by a colon (":"). A null directory name in CDPATH means the same thing as the current directory.
#The -P option means not to follow symbolic links: symbolic links are resolved while cd is traversing directory
#and before processing an instance of .. in directory.
#By default, or when the -L option is supplied, symbolic links in directory are resolved after cd processes an
#instance an instance of .. in directory. 
#If .. appears in directory, cd processes it by removing the immediately preceding pathname component, back to a 
#slash or the beginning of directory, and verifying that the portion of directory it has processed to that point is
# still a valid directory name after removing the pathname component. If it is not a valid direcotry name, cd returns
#a non-zero status.
#If -e option given with -P and cd cannot successfully determine the current working directory after a successful 
#directory change, it returns a non-zero status.
#On systems that support it, the -@ option presents the extended attributes associated with a file as a directory.
#if directory is '-', it is converted to $OLDPWD before attempting the directory change.
##if cd uses a non-empty directory anme from CDPATH or if '-' is the first argument, and the directory change is
# successful, cd writes the absolute pathname of the new working direcotry to the standard output.
#if the directory change is successful, cd sets the value of the PWD environment variable to the new directory
#name, and sets the OLDPWD environment variable to the value of the current working directory before the change.

#return status is zero if the directory is successfully changed, non-zero otherwise.

